 "issue": 1747,
 "issue_title": "DX: clarify from_dict nested object error message",
 "issue_body": "## Summary\n`ar.from_dict()` has a malformed error message when a nested dictionary is provided as a column value.\n\n## Why it matters\nThis is a small developer-experience bug, but it affects one of the beginner-friendly constructors. Clear validation messages are important because `from_dict()` is often used in examples, tests, and quick experimentation.\n\n## Evidence\n- `arnio/convert.py` raises `ValueError(f\"Nested objects are not supported in column{col_name}\")`.\n- Because there is no separator or quoting around the column name, an input such as `{\"meta\": {\"a\": 1}}` produces an error like `Nested objects are not supported in columnmeta`.\n- Existing `from_dict()` tests cover several invalid inputs, but there is no assertion for this nested-dictionary message.\n\n## Reproduction or trigger\n1. Call `ar.from_dict({\"meta\": {\"a\": 1}})`.\n2. Observe the raised `ValueError` message.\n\n## Expected behavior\nThe message should clearly identify the column, for example: `Nested objects are not supported in column 'meta'`.\n\n## Actual behavior\nThe current message joins the word `column` and the column name without spacing or quoting.\n\n## Suggested direction\nUpdate the error message in `arnio/convert.py` and add a focused regression test in `tests/test_frame.py` or another existing `from_dict()` test module.\n\n## References\n- `arnio/convert.py`\n- `tests/test_frame.py`\r\n",
 "issue_url": "https://github.com/im-anishraj/arnio/issues/1747",
 "pr": 1857,
 "pr_title": "clarify from_dict error message",
 "pr_body": "## Summary\r\n\r\n1. Updated convert.py so from_dict raises a clearer error for nested dictionary values:\r\n\r\n-Nested objects are not supported in column 'meta'\r\n .Added regression coverage in [test_frame.py] for both ar.from_dict and ar.Arframe.from_dict\r\n\r\n## Linked issue\r\nfixes #1747 \r\n\r\n## Type of change\r\n- [ ] Bug fix\r\n\r\n## Area\r\n\r\n- [ ] Python API / ArFrame\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\n- [ ] `make test` (or `python -m pytest tests -v --cov=arnio`)\r\n- [ ] `make lint` (or run separately:\r\n  ```powershell\r\n  python -m ruff check .\r\n  python -m black --check .\r\n  ```\r\n)\r\n- [ ] optionally `python -m pytest tests -v --tb=short -x`\r\n- [ ] Other:\r\n\r\n- python -m py_compile convert.py tests/test_frame.py\r\n\r\n## GSSoC labels\r\n<!-- Maintainers only: use exact scoring labels where applicable, for example `gssoc:approved`, `level:beginner`, `quality:clean`, and `type:bug`. Do not add labels only to increase points. -->\r\n\r\n## Contributor checklist\r\n- [ ] I read the contributing guide.\r\n- [ ] I kept the PR focused on one issue or one logical change.\r\n- [ ] I added or updated tests for behavior changes.\r\n- [ ] I added or updated docs/examples for public API changes.\r\n- [ ] I checked that generated files, logs, and local build artifacts are not committed.\r\n- [ ] My PR title uses Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`).\r\n\r\n## Maintainer notes\r\nFix is intentionally narrow and only changes the text of the invalid-input error path plus the matching regression test.",
